==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[utilize]] {{qualifier|American}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr}} {{term|utiliser|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|it|en}} {{term|utilizzare|lang=it}}, from {{term|utile||useful|lang=it}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|utilis|ūtilis|lang=la}}, from {{term|uti|ūtī|use|lang=la}}. Attested c. 1810.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref>

===Verb===
{{en-verb|utilis|ing}}

# To make [[useful]], to find a practical use for.
# To make {{l|en|use}} of; to [[use]].
# To make [[best]] use of; to use to its fullest extent, potential, or ability.
# To [[make do]] with; to use in manner different from that originally [[intended]]<ref name="getting-the-words-right">T.A.R. Cheney, ''Getting the Words Right'', Writer's Digest Books (1983).</ref>

====Usage notes====
<!-- {{unreferenced}} Technically we do have references, but in some cases I doubt whether the references actually support our claim. -->
<!-- All the references I've given support the claim. Even the recently-added dictionary.com reference supports the claim in its usage notes. (Should this be in discussion? -->
* Many style guides have advised against ''utilize'' and ''[[utilise]]'', arguing that the simpler verb ''[[use]]'' is always preferable (and analogously, that the noun ''[[use]]'' is preferable to ''[[utilization]]'' and ''[[utilisation]]'').<ref>Sir Ernest Gowers 1965 ''The Complete Plain Words'' Oxford: Oxford University Press</ref> <ref>Eric Partridge 1973 ''Usage and Abusage: A Guide to Good English'' England: Penguin Books</ref> <ref>John E. Kahn (ed) 1985 ''The Right Word at the Right Time'' London:Readers Digest</ref> <ref>Pam Peters 1995 ''The Cambridge Australian English Style Guide'' Cambridge:Cambridge University Press</ref>
* Some have argued that {{term||use}} and {{term||utilize}}, when used in American English, are not quite synonymous, the latter having the additional implication of use outside a thing's intended purpose. By this argument, one can ''utilize'' a butter-knife to remove a flat-head screw, but a screwdriver can only be ''used'' for such.  "Use" may also be more general whereas "utilize" may apply to profitable or practical use.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com|use}}</ref><ref>{{R:American Heritage 2000}}</ref>

